{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held for ransom. He was released in 1172 after paying a large sum.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Reign and Conflicts**\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He opposed the expansionist policies of Amalric I of Jerusalem and later supported the rise of Baldwin IV. He served as regent for Baldwin IV from 1174 to 1177. Raymond III arranged Baldwin IVs marriage to Princess Theodora Komnene in an attempt to secure Byzantine alliances. He also mediated between factions in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1177 he led forces at the Battle of Montgisard where the Crusaders defeated Saladins army.\n\n**Relationship with Saladin**\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with Saladin. Despite being a Christian leader he maintained diplomatic relations with Saladin. In 1180 he signed a truce with Saladin to secure his northern frontier. This truce allowed him to focus on internal conflicts in the County of Tripoli. However by 1186 tensions rose as Saladin consolidated power in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Marriage and Succession**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures the heiress of the Principality of Galilee in 1174. Their marriage strengthened his political ties in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. However they had no children. Upon Raymond IIIs death in 1187 his cousin Raymond of Antioch became heir to Tripoli. His death marked the end of a significant era in Crusader politics.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and military leader. His regency during Baldwin IVs minority helped stabilize the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His ability to negotiate with both Christian and Muslim powers demonstrated his pragmatic approach to leadership. Despite his efforts the County of Tripoli faced increasing pressure from Saladin which culminated in its fall in 1187.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.2458229511976242}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -5.471556869451888e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.02704458311200142}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.386805812828243e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.3282245993614197}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.047730978578329086}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.3351351299206726e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00018809456378221512}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.3257717490196228}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.21438068151474}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -5.125986263010418e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00045658653834834695}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00046885941992513835}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6331539882230572e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00014518637908622622}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0015979153104126453}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00010346830822527409}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.42877426743507385}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.014259115792810917}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.00010346830822527409}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.0034650068264454603}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.618460560683161e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.3828182090946939e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -1.4185804502631072e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -0.00033241944038309157}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.005087288